\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Quality estimation framework.}
    Structural agreement signals reliably predict output quality for LLM-based NER: VC achieves $\rho{=}0.38$ on the medium-difficulty primary test bed (SciERC), while EM reaches AUROC${=}0.82$ on CoNLL.
    Signal rankings are difficulty-dependent but stable across model families and a 4B scale ablation, supporting practical applications such as selective prediction and error flagging.

    \item \textbf{Multi-causal diagnosis of the correlation-selection gap.}
    Despite reliable quality estimation, selection fails because two mechanisms jointly eliminate per-sample discriminability.
    Instance degeneracy: 21--58\% of instances produce identical F1 across all $N$ samples, eliminating selection headroom despite positive within-instance utility--F1 correlation (median $\rho{=}0.52$); on non-degenerate instances, consensus-proximity closes 23--41\% of the oracle gap.
    LP compression: the only per-sample signal has median within-instance range of 0.041 nats ($\rho(\text{LP}, \text{F1}){=}0.248$).
    A LoRA-rank ablation reduces degeneracy from 21\% to 7\% without enabling selection, confirming that low degeneracy is necessary but not sufficient; the non-tied LP subset ($+2.9$~pp, $p{=}0.004$) shows signals carry information that compression prevents from being exploited.

    \item \textbf{Diagnostic ablation chain.}
    A five-layer ablation (free-form decoding, entity-token LP, verbalized confidence, non-tied subset, signal combinations) confirms these mechanisms are intrinsic to capable fine-tuned models, not artifacts of constrained decoding or signal design. Signal rankings shift with task difficulty and collapse at the capability boundary; closing the gap likely requires learned per-sample scoring.
\end{enumerate}

